IMPORT prelude

-- Criminal Procedure Code 2010 (Singapore) -- what the Code means for a person suspected, arrested or accused of an offence: whether
-- the offence is arrestable or bailable (s 2(1)); which State Court may try it (ss 7, 8, 9); when the police may arrest without a
-- warrant (ss 64, 65) and how long they may hold the person (ss 65(4), 67, 68); release on bail or personal bond (ss 92, 93, 94, 95);
-- the duty to answer police questions and the cautioned statement (ss 22(2), 23(5), (6), 261); compounding (ss 241, 242); a
-- discharge by the Public Prosecutor (s 232); the most each court may sentence (s 303); caning (ss 325, 328); and appeal (ss 375,
-- 377(1), (2)).
--
-- SEAT: the accused person -- an individual suspected of, arrested for, charged with or convicted of an offence. Every decision is
-- phrased as that person would ask it. Where a provision addresses the police, the Public Prosecutor or a court, it is encoded as
-- what may be done to the accused, what the accused must be given, and by when.
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, version in force from 15/9/2026, as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/CPC2010.txt.
--
-- NOT ENCODED. The First Schedule (which says, offence by offence under the Penal Code 1871, whether it is arrestable, bailable and
-- which court tries it): its answers are inputs here, stated as facts the person can read off the Schedule. The Fourth Schedule
-- (offences compoundable by the victim): an input. The Criminal Procedure Rules (the grounds on which a court may refuse bail, and the
-- conditions for releasing an extradition arrestee): not deposited, so the court's belief is an input. Every other Part: the
-- Public Prosecutor's consent to prosecution (s 10), investigation powers beyond s 22(2) and s 23, warrants, summonses and
-- searches, the criminal case disclosure procedures, charges, trial procedure, evidence (Part 14 beyond s 261), community sentences,
-- reformative training, the presumptive minimum sentence (s 303A), appeals beyond who may appeal what and when, criminal revision,
-- and the machinery (Part 3's offices, rule-making powers, the Schedules' amendment). s 93(3) and (3B) (release pending further
-- investigation of some other bailable offence) are summarised in a comment, not separated as their own limb.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the individual to
-- whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass,
-- no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Criminal Procedure Code 2010 -- the accused person`

§§ `2(1) -- arrestable and bailable offences`

-- s 2(1): "'arrestable offence' and 'arrestable case' mean, respectively, an offence for which and a case in which a police officer
-- may ordinarily arrest without warrant according to the third column of the First Schedule or under any other written law".
-- Gloss: an offence the police may arrest you for without first getting a warrant.
GIVEN `the First Schedule's third column says the police may ordinarily arrest without warrant` IS A BOOLEAN
      `another written law lets the police arrest without warrant for it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my offence arrestable` IF
        `the First Schedule's third column says the police may ordinarily arrest without warrant`
    OR  `another written law lets the police arrest without warrant for it`

-- s 2(1): "'bailable offence' means an offence shown as bailable in the fifth column of the First Schedule or which is made bailable
-- by any other written law, and 'non-bailable offence' means any offence other than a bailable offence".
-- Gloss: an offence for which you are ordinarily entitled to be let out on bail.
GIVEN `the First Schedule's fifth column shows it as bailable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `another written law makes it bailable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my offence bailable` IF
        `the First Schedule's fifth column shows it as bailable`
    OR  `another written law makes it bailable`

§§ `7, 8, 9 -- which State Court may try me`

-- s 2(1): "'fine-only offence' means an offence that is punishable only with a fine".
-- The facts about the offence that ss 7, 8 and 9 read. The seventh column of the First Schedule (Penal Code 1871 offences) and the
-- law creating any other offence are read together as one fact each: ss 9(2)(a), (b) and 9(3)(a), (b) give the same answer.
DECLARE `My offence` HAS
    `the most years of imprisonment provided by law` IS A NUMBER
    `it is punishable with imprisonment for life` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is punishable with death` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is punishable only with a fine` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the First Schedule or the law creating it shows it triable by a Magistrate's Court` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the First Schedule or the law creating it shows it triable by a District Court` IS A BOOLEAN

-- Reading taken (an inference, the text does not say): an offence punishable with death or with imprisonment for life does not have
-- a "maximum term of imprisonment" that "does not exceed" 5 or 10 years. s 2(1) defines "life imprisonment" as imprisonment "for the
-- duration of a person's natural life", not a term of years.
GIVEN o IS A `My offence`
      `the limit in years` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `its maximum term is within` IF
        NOT o's `it is punishable with death`
    AND NOT o's `it is punishable with imprisonment for life`
    AND o's `the most years of imprisonment provided by law` AT MOST `the limit in years`

-- s 8(1): "District Courts have jurisdiction and power to try any offence for which the maximum term of imprisonment provided by law
-- does not exceed 10 years or which is a fine-only offence."
-- s 9(3): "Despite section 8(1), a District Court may try any offence other than an offence punishable with death -- (a) ... shown to
-- be triable by a District Court in the seventh column of the First Schedule; (b) ... shown to be triable by a District Court under
-- that law; or (c) if the Public Prosecutor applies to the District Court to try such offence, and the accused consents".
GIVEN o IS A `My offence`
      `the Public Prosecutor applied to the District Court and I consented` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May a District Court try me` IF
        `its maximum term is within` o 10
    OR  o's `it is punishable only with a fine`
    OR  (    NOT o's `it is punishable with death`
         AND (   o's `the First Schedule or the law creating it shows it triable by a District Court`
              OR `the Public Prosecutor applied to the District Court and I consented`))

-- s 7(1)(a): Magistrates' Courts may "try any offence for which the maximum term of imprisonment provided by law does not exceed 5
-- years or which is a fine-only offence". s 9(2): "Despite section 7(1), a Magistrate's Court may try any offence" shown triable by
-- one in the seventh column of the First Schedule or under the law creating it. s 9(1): "Where an offence is triable by a District
-- Court but not by a Magistrate's Court, the Public Prosecutor may in writing authorise a Magistrate's Court in any particular case
-- to try the offence."
-- Reading taken (an inference): "triable by a District Court" in s 9(1) is read without the s 9(3)(c) consent route.
GIVEN o IS A `My offence`
      `the Public Prosecutor has authorised a Magistrate's Court in writing to try my case` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May a Magistrate's Court try me` IF
        `its maximum term is within` o 5
    OR  o's `it is punishable only with a fine`
    OR  o's `the First Schedule or the law creating it shows it triable by a Magistrate's Court`
    OR  (    `May a District Court try me` o FALSE
         AND `the Public Prosecutor has authorised a Magistrate's Court in writing to try my case`)

§§ `303 -- the most each court may sentence me to`

DECLARE Court IS ONE OF
    `the General Division of the High Court`
    `a District Court`
    `a Magistrate's Court`

-- s 303(1): "The General Division of the High Court may pass any sentence authorised by law." (2): "Subject to this Code and any
-- other written law, a District Court may pass any of the following sentences: (a) imprisonment not exceeding 10 years; (b) fine not
-- exceeding $30,000; (c) caning not exceeding 12 strokes". (3): a Magistrate's Court "(a) imprisonment not exceeding 3 years; (b)
-- fine not exceeding $10,000; (c) caning not exceeding 6 strokes".
-- The High Court has no ceiling in s 303 beyond what the law creating the offence allows, so the answer for it is NOTHING: the
-- ceiling is the offence's own, which this encoding does not hold. "Subject to ... any other written law": another Act may give a
-- State Court more; that is not encoded.
GIVEN `the court trying me` IS A Court
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most imprisonment in years this court can give me` IS
    CONSIDER `the court trying me`
    WHEN `a District Court` THEN JUST 10
    WHEN `a Magistrate's Court` THEN JUST 3
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

GIVEN `the court trying me` IS A Court
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most fine this court can give me` IS
    CONSIDER `the court trying me`
    WHEN `a District Court` THEN JUST 30000
    WHEN `a Magistrate's Court` THEN JUST 10000
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

GIVEN `the court trying me` IS A Court
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most strokes of the cane this court can give me` IS
    CONSIDER `the court trying me`
    WHEN `a District Court` THEN JUST 12
    WHEN `a Magistrate's Court` THEN JUST 6
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

§§ `64 -- arrest without a warrant`

-- s 64(1): "Any police officer may, without a warrant, arrest any person who -- (a) has been concerned in an arrestable offence or is
-- reasonably suspected of having been involved in one, or against whom a reasonable complaint has been made or credible information
-- has been received ...; (b) possesses a housebreaking tool without being able to provide a lawful excuse ...; (c) has been
-- proclaimed as an offender under section 88; (d) possesses anything that may reasonably be suspected to be stolen or fraudulently
-- obtained property, and who may reasonably be suspected of having committed an offence in acquiring it; (e) obstructs a police
-- officer while the police officer is doing his or her duty, or has escaped or tries to escape from lawful custody; (f) is
-- reasonably suspected of being a deserter ...; (g) is trying to conceal his or her presence in circumstances that suggest he or
-- she is doing so with a view to committing an arrestable offence; (h) has no apparent means of subsistence or who cannot give a
-- satisfactory account of himself or herself; (i) is known to be a habitual robber, housebreaker or thief, or a habitual receiver
-- ... or ... to habitually commit extortion ...; (j) commits or attempts to commit a breach of the peace in the police officer's
-- presence; (k) is known to be planning to commit an arrestable offence, if it appears to the police officer that the offence
-- cannot otherwise be prevented; (l) is subject to police supervision and who has failed to comply with this Code or any other
-- written law; or (m) has breached any detention order under any written law."
-- (2): "This section does not affect any other law empowering a police officer to arrest without a warrant."
DECLARE `Why the officer is arresting me` IS ONE OF
    `I am concerned in, or reasonably suspected of, the offence`
    `I have a housebreaking tool and cannot give a lawful excuse`
    `I have been proclaimed an offender under section 88`
    `I have property reasonably suspected to be stolen and am suspected of an offence in getting it`
    `I obstructed a police officer on duty, or escaped or tried to escape custody`
    `I am reasonably suspected of being a deserter`
    `I was concealing my presence with a view to an arrestable offence`
    `I have no apparent means of subsistence or cannot give a satisfactory account of myself`
    `I am known to be a habitual robber, housebreaker, thief, receiver or extortioner`
    `I committed or attempted a breach of the peace in the officer's presence`
    `I am known to be planning an arrestable offence that cannot otherwise be prevented`
    `I am under police supervision and failed to comply with the law`
    `I breached a detention order`
    `none of these`

GIVEN `the ground` IS A `Why the officer is arresting me`
      `the offence is arrestable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `another written law lets the police arrest me without a warrant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May a police officer arrest me without a warrant` IF
        `another written law lets the police arrest me without a warrant`
    OR  CONSIDER `the ground`
        WHEN `I am concerned in, or reasonably suspected of, the offence` THEN `the offence is arrestable`
        WHEN `none of these` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `65 -- arrest for refusing my name and address`

-- s 65(1): "A police officer may arrest any person who is accused of committing, or who commits in the view or presence of the police
-- officer, a non-arrestable offence if, on the demand of the police officer, the person refuses to give his or her name, residential
-- address or unique identification number." (2): "A police officer may arrest such a person who gives a residential address outside
-- Singapore, or a name, residential address or unique identification number which the police officer has reason to believe is
-- false." (6): "unique identification number" is an identity card, passport or similar number (or a minor's birth certificate
-- number). The officer's "reason to believe" is a judgment for the officer: an input.
GIVEN `I am accused of, or committed in the officer's presence, a non-arrestable offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I refused to give my name, residential address or identification number when asked` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I gave a residential address outside Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the officer has reason to believe what I gave is false` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May a police officer arrest me for not giving my particulars` IF
        `I am accused of, or committed in the officer's presence, a non-arrestable offence`
    AND (   `I refused to give my name, residential address or identification number when asked`
         OR `I gave a residential address outside Singapore`
         OR `the officer has reason to believe what I gave is false`)

§§ `65(4), 67, 68 -- how long I can be held before a Magistrate`

-- s 67: "A police officer making an arrest without warrant must, without unnecessary delay and subject to the provisions of this Code
-- on bail or previous release, take or send the person arrested before a Magistrate's Court."
-- s 68(1): "Unless the court orders otherwise under section 92(3)(a) or 93(3B)(a), no police officer may detain in custody a person
-- who has been arrested without a warrant for a longer period than under all the circumstances of the case is reasonable." (2): "The
-- period must not exceed 48 hours exclusive of the time necessary for the journey from the place of arrest to the Magistrate's
-- Court."
-- s 65(4): a person arrested under s 65 who "refuses or is unable to sign the bond ... must, within 24 hours of the arrest (excluding
-- the time necessary for the journey to a Magistrate's Court), be brought before a Magistrate's Court".
-- Where the court has ordered otherwise under s 92(3)(a) or 93(3B)(a), the Code sets no figure in s 68, so the answer is NOTHING:
-- not covered by this encoding.
GIVEN `I was arrested under section 65 and refused or could not sign the bond` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the hours needed for the journey to the Magistrate's Court` IS A NUMBER
      `the court has ordered under section 92(3)(a) or 93(3B)(a) that I not be released` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most hours I can be held before being brought before a Magistrate's Court` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `I was arrested under section 65 and refused or could not sign the bond`
            THEN JUST (24 PLUS `the hours needed for the journey to the Magistrate's Court`)
        IF `the court has ordered under section 92(3)(a) or 93(3B)(a) that I not be released`
            THEN NOTHING
        OTHERWISE JUST (48 PLUS `the hours needed for the journey to the Magistrate's Court`)

-- s 68(1) also caps the detention at what is "reasonable" "under all the circumstances of the case": a judgment the Code does not
-- give to a named person, asked here as a fact.
GIVEN `the hours I have been held since arrest without a warrant` IS A NUMBER
      `the hours spent on the journey to the Magistrate's Court` IS A NUMBER
      `the period is reasonable in all the circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my detention without a warrant still within the limit` IF
        `the period is reasonable in all the circumstances`
    AND `the hours I have been held since arrest without a warrant` MINUS `the hours spent on the journey to the Magistrate's Court` AT MOST 48

-- The same duty as a regulative rule, from the arresting officer's side, so that a trace can show it kept or broken.
DECLARE `Who acts` IS ONE OF
    `the police officer holding me`
    `me`

DECLARE `What is done` IS ONE OF
    `bring me before a Magistrate's Court`

GIVEN `the hours needed for the journey to the Magistrate's Court` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Who acts` `What is done`
DECIDE `the police must bring me before a Magistrate within 48 hours` IS
    PARTY `the police officer holding me`
    MUST  `bring me before a Magistrate's Court`
    WITHIN 48 PLUS `the hours needed for the journey to the Magistrate's Court`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  BREACH BY `the police officer holding me`

§§ `92, 93, 95 -- bail`

-- s 92(1): "When any person, except a person accused of a non-bailable offence -- (a) is arrested or detained without warrant by a
-- police officer, or appears or is brought before a court; and (b) is prepared to give bail ..., the person must be released on bail".
-- (2)(a): the police or court may instead release the person on a personal bond without sureties.
-- (3): where the offence "is not a fine-only offence, and a court believes, on any ground prescribed in the Criminal Procedure
-- Rules, that the person, if released, will not surrender to custody, be available for investigations or attend court", the court
-- may order the police not to release the person, or refuse release.
-- s 93(1): "Subject to section 95(1)", a person accused of a non-bailable offence "may be released on bail by a police officer of or
-- above the rank of sergeant or by the court". (2): "if ... there are no reasonable grounds for believing that the accused has
-- committed a non-bailable offence, the police officer or court must release the accused". [(3) and (3B): where there are grounds
-- for further investigation of some other bailable offence the accused must be released on bail or personal bond, subject to the
-- same court power as s 92(3); not separated here.]
-- s 95(1): "An accused must not be released on bail or on personal bond if -- (a) the accused is charged for an offence punishable
-- with death or imprisonment for life; (b) the accused is accused of any non-bailable offence, and the court believes, on any ground
-- prescribed in the Criminal Procedure Rules, that the accused, if released, will not surrender to custody, be available for
-- investigations or attend court; or (c) the accused has been arrested or taken into custody under a warrant issued under section 12
-- or 34 of the Extradition Act 1968 or endorsed under section 33 of that Act." (2): "the court may -- (a) direct that any juvenile or
-- any sick or infirm person accused of such an offence be released on bail; (b) release on bail an accused charged with an offence
-- mentioned in subsection (1)(a), if -- (i) the offence is also punishable with an alternative punishment other than death or life
-- imprisonment; and (ii) the offence is to be tried before a District Court or a Magistrate's Court; or (c) release on bail an accused
-- who has been arrested ... under a warrant mentioned in subsection (1)(c), if the conditions prescribed in the Criminal Procedure
-- Rules for such release are satisfied."
-- Reading taken (an inference): s 95(2)(a) "accused of such an offence" is read as reaching (1)(a) and (1)(b), which name offences,
-- and not (1)(c), which names a warrant.
-- The court's belief and the Rules' conditions are judgments for the court: inputs. s 92(1)(b), being "prepared to give bail", is
-- taken as given.
DECLARE `My bail facts` HAS
    `my offence is bailable` IS A BOOLEAN
    `my offence is punishable only with a fine` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am charged with an offence punishable with death or imprisonment for life` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it also has another punishment and is to be tried in a District Court or Magistrate's Court` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am a juvenile, or sick or infirm` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am held under an Extradition Act 1968 warrant` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the court is satisfied the Rules' conditions for releasing me under that warrant are met` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the court believes on a prescribed ground I will not surrender, be available or attend court` IS A BOOLEAN
    `there are reasonable grounds to believe I committed the non-bailable offence` IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `Bail position` IS ONE OF
    `I must be released on bail or personal bond`
    `the court may refuse to release me`
    `I may be released on bail by a sergeant or above, or by the court`
    `only the court may release me on bail`
    `I must not be released on bail or personal bond`

GIVEN b IS A `My bail facts`
GIVETH A `Bail position`
DECIDE `What is my position on bail` IS
    BRANCH
        IF b's `I am charged with an offence punishable with death or imprisonment for life`
            THEN IF     b's `it also has another punishment and is to be tried in a District Court or Magistrate's Court`
                     OR b's `I am a juvenile, or sick or infirm`
                 THEN `only the court may release me on bail`
                 ELSE `I must not be released on bail or personal bond`
        IF b's `I am held under an Extradition Act 1968 warrant`
            THEN IF b's `the court is satisfied the Rules' conditions for releasing me under that warrant are met`
                 THEN `only the court may release me on bail`
                 ELSE `I must not be released on bail or personal bond`
        IF b's `my offence is bailable`
            THEN IF     NOT b's `my offence is punishable only with a fine`
                    AND b's `the court believes on a prescribed ground I will not surrender, be available or attend court`
                 THEN `the court may refuse to release me`
                 ELSE `I must be released on bail or personal bond`
        IF b's `the court believes on a prescribed ground I will not surrender, be available or attend court`
            THEN IF b's `I am a juvenile, or sick or infirm`
                 THEN `only the court may release me on bail`
                 ELSE `I must not be released on bail or personal bond`
        IF NOT b's `there are reasonable grounds to believe I committed the non-bailable offence`
            THEN `I must be released on bail or personal bond`
        OTHERWISE `I may be released on bail by a sergeant or above, or by the court`

§§ `92(2), 93(1A) to (1D) -- release on my own personal bond`

-- s 92(2)(a): for a bailable offence "the police officer or the court may, instead of taking bail from the person, release the person
-- if the person signs a personal bond without sureties".
-- s 93(1A): for a non-bailable offence, the court may release the accused on personal bond if "(a) the non-bailable offence ... is
-- an offence punishable with imprisonment for not more than 7 years; and (b) the prosecution consents". (1C): a police officer "of
-- or above the rank of sergeant" may do so on the 7-year condition alone. (1D): neither applies "where the non-bailable offence ...
-- is an offence punishable with imprisonment for more than 7 years due to the application of an enhanced penalty provision".
-- Illustration: Penal Code s 324 read with s 74A carries 14 years, so A "is not eligible to be released on his or her own personal
-- bond". Gloss: a personal bond is your own promise to come back, with no surety.
DECLARE `Who is releasing me` IS ONE OF
    `the court`
    `a police officer of or above the rank of sergeant`
    `a police officer below the rank of sergeant`

GIVEN `my offence is bailable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the most years of imprisonment for my offence, counting any enhanced penalty` IS A NUMBER
      `who is releasing me` IS A `Who is releasing me`
      `the prosecution consents` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I be released on my own personal bond instead of bail` IF
        `my offence is bailable`
    OR  (    `the most years of imprisonment for my offence, counting any enhanced penalty` AT MOST 7
         AND CONSIDER `who is releasing me`
             WHEN `the court` THEN `the prosecution consents`
             WHEN `a police officer of or above the rank of sergeant` THEN TRUE
             OTHERWISE FALSE)

§§ `94 -- the conditions I am released on`

-- s 94(1): "All of the following conditions are imposed when a police officer or the court grants bail or releases the accused on
-- personal bond under section 92 or 93, unless the police officer or the court (as the case may be) specifies otherwise: (a) the
-- accused must surrender the accused's travel document; (b) ... surrender to custody, be available for investigations, or attend
-- court ...; (c) ... not commit any offence while released ...; (d) ... not interfere with any witness or otherwise obstruct the
-- course of justice". (3), (4): the conditions may include electronic monitoring, and "if the prosecution applies ... the police
-- officer or court must" impose it.
GIVEN `the police or court specified otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I surrender my travel document` IF
    NOT `the police or court specified otherwise`

GIVEN `the prosecution applied for electronic monitoring` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the police or court imposed it of their own accord` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must my whereabouts be electronically monitored` IF
        `the prosecution applied for electronic monitoring`
    OR  `the police or court imposed it of their own accord`

§§ `22(2) -- must I answer the police`

-- s 22(2): "The person examined is bound to state truly what the person knows of the facts and circumstances of the case, except that
-- the person need not say anything that might expose the person to a criminal charge, penalty or forfeiture."
GIVEN `I am being examined in a police investigation as someone acquainted with the facts` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a true answer might expose me to a criminal charge, penalty or forfeiture` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I answer this question truly` IF
        `I am being examined in a police investigation as someone acquainted with the facts`
    AND NOT `a true answer might expose me to a criminal charge, penalty or forfeiture`

§§ `23, 261 -- the cautioned statement and my silence`

-- s 23(1): on being charged, or informed that the accused may be prosecuted, the accused "must be served with and have read to the
-- accused a written notice", warning that "If you keep quiet now about any fact or matter in your defence and you reveal this fact or
-- matter in your defence only at your trial, the judge may be less likely to believe you." (3): the statement is recorded "(a) in
-- writing; or (b) in the form of an audiovisual recording".
-- (5): if in writing, "a copy of the statement must be given to the accused at the end of the recording". (6): if audiovisual, "(a)
-- if requested by the defence, arrangements must be made for the accused and the accused's advocate (if any) to view the audiovisual
-- recording ... as soon as practicable"; "(b) if a transcript ... is made, a copy of the transcript must be given to the accused".
DECLARE `How my statement was recorded` IS ONE OF
    `in writing`
    `as an audiovisual recording`

GIVEN `how it was recorded` IS A `How my statement was recorded`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I be given a copy of my cautioned statement at the end of the recording` IF
    CONSIDER `how it was recorded`
    WHEN `in writing` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `how it was recorded` IS A `How my statement was recorded`
      `my defence asked to view it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I be allowed to view the recording of my cautioned statement` IF
        `my defence asked to view it`
    AND CONSIDER `how it was recorded`
        WHEN `as an audiovisual recording` THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 261(1): where the accused, "on being charged with an offence, or informed ... that the accused may be prosecuted ..., failed to
-- mention any fact which the accused subsequently relies on in the accused's defence, being a fact which in the circumstances existing
-- at the time the accused could reasonably have been expected to mention", the court "may ... draw such inferences from the failure as
-- appear proper". The "reasonably ... expected" judgment is the court's: an input.
GIVEN `I was charged, or told I may be prosecuted` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I did not then mention a fact` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I now rely on that fact in my defence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court finds I could reasonably have been expected to mention it then` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the court draw an inference against me from my silence` IF
        `I was charged, or told I may be prosecuted`
    AND `I did not then mention a fact`
    AND `I now rely on that fact in my defence`
    AND `the court finds I could reasonably have been expected to mention it then`

§§ `241, 242 -- compounding`

-- s 242(1): "The Public Prosecutor may ... at any time compound any offence or class of offences as may be prescribed by collecting
-- from a person who is reasonably suspected of having committed the offence a sum of money not exceeding -- (a) one half of the
-- amount of the maximum fine that is prescribed for the offence; or (b) $5,000, whichever is the lower."
-- Gloss: compounding settles the offence for a payment, without a conviction. No prescribing instrument is deposited: whether the
-- offence is prescribed is an input. NOTHING means the offence is not compoundable under s 242.
GIVEN `my offence is prescribed as compoundable under section 242` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the maximum fine prescribed for my offence` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most the Public Prosecutor can collect to compound my offence` IS
    IF `my offence is prescribed as compoundable under section 242`
    THEN JUST `the lower amount`
    ELSE NOTHING
    WHERE
        `half the fine` MEANS `the maximum fine prescribed for my offence` DIVIDED BY 2
        `the lower amount` MEANS IF `half the fine` AT MOST 5000 THEN `half the fine` ELSE 5000

-- s 241(1): an offence in the third column of the Fourth Schedule "may be compounded at any time by the person specified in the fourth
-- column". (2): "where investigations have commenced ..., or when the accused has been charged in court for the offence, the offence
-- may only be compounded with the consent of the Public Prosecutor".
GIVEN `my offence is in the Fourth Schedule` IS A BOOLEAN
      `investigations have begun or I have been charged` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the person named in the Fourth Schedule compound my offence without the Public Prosecutor's consent` IF
        `my offence is in the Fourth Schedule`
    AND NOT `investigations have begun or I have been charged`

-- s 241(4): "Where investigations have commenced for an offence which is subsequently compounded under subsection (2), no further
-- proceedings are to be taken against the person reasonably suspected". (5): after the accused has been charged in court, "the court
-- must order a discharge amounting to an acquittal". s 242(3): the same no-further-proceedings rule; (4): after charge,
-- "such composition has the effect of an acquittal". Neither section states the effect of compounding before investigations begin:
-- that case is `not stated by the Code`.
DECLARE `When it was compounded` IS ONE OF
    `before investigations began`
    `after investigations began, before I was charged`
    `after I was charged in court`

DECLARE `Who compounded it` IS ONE OF
    `the person named in the Fourth Schedule`
    `the Public Prosecutor under section 242`

DECLARE `Effect of compounding` IS ONE OF
    `no further proceedings may be taken against me`
    `the court must order a discharge amounting to an acquittal`
    `it has the effect of an acquittal`
    `not stated by the Code`

GIVEN `when` IS A `When it was compounded`
      `who` IS A `Who compounded it`
GIVETH AN `Effect of compounding`
DECIDE `What happens to my case once my offence is compounded` IS
    CONSIDER `when`
    WHEN `before investigations began` THEN `not stated by the Code`
    WHEN `after investigations began, before I was charged` THEN `no further proceedings may be taken against me`
    WHEN `after I was charged in court` THEN
        CONSIDER `who`
        WHEN `the person named in the Fourth Schedule` THEN `the court must order a discharge amounting to an acquittal`
        WHEN `the Public Prosecutor under section 242` THEN `it has the effect of an acquittal`

§§ `232 -- discharge when the Public Prosecutor drops the charge`

-- s 232(1): the Public Prosecutor may inform the court that he "will not further prosecute the accused upon the charge", and the
-- accused "must be discharged". (2): "Except in cases referred to in section 147, a discharge under subsection (1) does not amount to
-- an acquittal unless the court so directs." (3), (4), (5): later, on the Public Prosecutor's application, a State Court may grant a
-- discharge amounting to an acquittal (or one having "the effect of an acquittal"). Gloss: a discharge not amounting to an acquittal
-- leaves you open to being charged again.
GIVEN `the court directed that it amount to an acquittal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my case is one referred to in section 147` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a court later granted the Public Prosecutor's application under section 232(3) or (4)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does my discharge amount to an acquittal` IF
        `the court directed that it amount to an acquittal`
    OR  `my case is one referred to in section 147`
    OR  `a court later granted the Public Prosecutor's application under section 232(3) or (4)`

§§ `325, 328 -- caning`

-- s 325(1): "The following persons shall not be punished with caning: (a) women; (b) men who are more than 50 years of age at the time
-- of infliction of the caning; (c) men sentenced to death whose sentences have not been commuted."
GIVEN `I am a woman` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my age when the caning would be carried out` IS A NUMBER
      `I am sentenced to death and it has not been commuted` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be caned` IF
        NOT `I am a woman`
    AND `my age when the caning would be carried out` AT MOST 50
    AND NOT `I am sentenced to death and it has not been commuted`

-- s 328(1): where an accused is sentenced at the same sitting for 2 or more offences punishable by caning, "the aggregate sentence of
-- caning ... must not exceed the specified limit". (6): "the specified limit is 24 strokes in the case of an adult and 10 strokes in
-- the case of a juvenile". s 330(2) sets the same figures for strokes "at any one time".
GIVEN `I am a juvenile` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most strokes I can be given at one sitting` IS
    IF `I am a juvenile` THEN 10 ELSE 24

-- s 325(2): where the person cannot be caned "because subsection (1)(a) or (b) applies, the court may ... impose a term of
-- imprisonment of not more than 12 months in lieu of the caning". s 328(2): where the aggregate would exceed the limit, "a term of
-- imprisonment of not more than 12 months in lieu of all such strokes which exceed the specified limit". ss 325(5), 328(5): neither
-- power applies "in relation to any offence which is committed before 2 January 2011".
-- Whether the two 12-month powers can both be used against one person is not said: that case is NOTHING, not covered by this
-- encoding. A man sentenced to death (s 325(1)(c)) gets no term in lieu.
GIVEN `I cannot be caned because I am a woman or over 50` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the strokes I would be given exceed the limit` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my offence was committed on or after 2 January 2011` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most extra imprisonment in months I can be given in lieu of caning` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `my offence was committed on or after 2 January 2011` THEN JUST 0
        IF `I cannot be caned because I am a woman or over 50` AND `the strokes I would be given exceed the limit` THEN NOTHING
        IF `I cannot be caned because I am a woman or over 50` OR `the strokes I would be given exceed the limit` THEN JUST 12
        OTHERWISE JUST 0

§§ `375, 377 -- appeal`

-- s 377(1): a party "not satisfied with any judgment, sentence or order of a trial court ... may appeal ... in respect of any error in
-- law or in fact, or in an appeal against sentence, on the ground that the sentence imposed is manifestly excessive or manifestly
-- inadequate". s 375: "An accused who has pleaded guilty and has been convicted on that plea in accordance with this Code may appeal
-- only against the extent or legality of the sentence."
DECLARE `What I may appeal against` IS ONE OF
    `only the extent or legality of my sentence`
    `the judgment, sentence or order, for an error of law or fact, or a manifestly excessive sentence`

GIVEN `I pleaded guilty and was convicted on that plea` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `What I may appeal against`
DECIDE `What may I appeal against` IS
    IF `I pleaded guilty and was convicted on that plea`
    THEN `only the extent or legality of my sentence`
    ELSE `the judgment, sentence or order, for an error of law or fact, or a manifestly excessive sentence`

-- s 377(2): the notice of appeal must be lodged "(a) in the case of an appeal against conviction, or an appeal against conviction and
-- sentence, within 14 days after the date of the sentence; and (b) in any other case, within 14 days after the date of the judgment,
-- sentence or order". It goes to the Registrar of the State Courts from a District Court or Magistrate's Court, and to the Registrar
-- of the Supreme Court from the High Court.
DECLARE `The date my 14 days run from` IS ONE OF
    `the date of the sentence`
    `the date of the judgment, sentence or order appealed against`

GIVEN `my appeal is against my conviction, with or without my sentence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `The date my 14 days run from`
DECIDE `From what date do my 14 days to lodge a notice of appeal run` IS
    IF `my appeal is against my conviction, with or without my sentence`
    THEN `the date of the sentence`
    ELSE `the date of the judgment, sentence or order appealed against`
